{"t":1791985389,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"6c72c607-61cb-4d8e-be97-634cf6e78a1e","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-14T07:39:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 140739\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n339 AM EDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nMade some slight adjustments to increase temps for today while\ndecreasing the dew points, in anticipation of a record warm\nOctober day.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Record warmth expected today, with a few strong \n  thunderstorms possible.\n\n- 2) Widespread frost/freeze likely this weekend, especially \n  west of I-95.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Record warmth expected today, with a few strong\nthunderstorms possible.\n\nMid-level ridging centered over the Deep South today as a cold front \nslowly approaches our area from the OH Valley. Near-record to record \nwarmth is expected today as highs reach well into the 80s. It is \npossible a couple locations could hit 90F. See the climate section \nbelow for a list of today's record highs. \n\nThe cold front reach the Alleghenies this afternoon, the slides down \ninto the area tonight. There will be some instability given the very \nwarm temperatures and shear values should be able to support \norganized thunderstorms, particularly along the front itself. A \nsevere storm or two with damaging wind gusts as the primary threat \nare possible, mainly west of the Blue Ridge. \n\nThe front stalls over the region through Thursday morning, keeping \nconditions much cooler and showery through Thursday evening. The \nfrontal boundary washes out Thursday night into Friday as high \npressure moves offshore of southern New England, which in turn \nincreases southerly winds over the Mid-Atlantic. Temperatures shoot \nback up to the low 80s Friday afternoon. \n\nA powerful cold front begins tracking across the region; the \nFall season's first true cold front. Gusty showers and a few \nthunderstorms are possible Friday afternoon. It will get very \nbreezy Friday afternoon as mid-level heights quickly fall and \nthe surface pressure gradient tightens in response to a \ndeepening low tracking to our north. Peak wind gusts of 35-45 \nmph are possible in the mountains, and 30-40 mph elsewhere. Wind\nAdvisories may be needed for some portion of the Alleghenies \nand Blue Ridge Friday afternoon into Friday night. Showers and\nsome thunderstorms are expected Friday afternoon ahead of the\nfront. If thunderstorms can tap into the increasing winds aloft,\nthere certainly could be a few strong to severe wind gusts.\n\nA potent upper trough passes through the region Friday night, \nbringing a reinforcing cold front that pushes out any lingering \nprecip.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Widespread frost/freeze likely this weekend, \nespecially west of I-95.\n\nAfter such a warm start to the fall, the cold truly will come \ncrashing in. Temps plummet to the 40s, with low to mid 30s in \nthe mountains, come sunrise Saturday. Wind chills in 20s for the\nAlleghenies, and 30s to low 40s elsewhere.\n\nNW'ly winds will gradually decrease Sat, with temps only forecast to \nclimb into the 50s (40s mtns). High pressure progged to build \noverhead Sat night, resulting in clear skies and calm winds within \nwhat will already be a cool background environment. This will be a \nnear ideal situation for radiational cooling. With Tds forecast to \nbe in the 20s and lower 30s, it appears as though a freeze may be \npossible for many locations, especially to the west of the Blue \nRidge. At least frost appears likely just about everywhere, and \nfreezing temps could encompass the entire area outside of the \nimmediate downtown areas in DC/Baltimore if cooler solutions were to \nverify. Frost/Freeze headlines will likely eventually be needed for \nmuch of the forecast area. Would not be surprised if temps \nsignificantly go lower than guidance given this very favorable \npattern. For context, the mountains typically have their first \nfreeze Oct 11, Shenandoah Valley area Oct 21, and east of Blue Ridge \nNov 1. \n\nQuiet weather conditions and a gradual warming trend in temperatures \nare expected for Sunday and Monday as high pressure moves offshore. \nHigh on Sunday and Monday will generally be in the 60s (50s \nmountains), with overnight lows in the 30s and 40s.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /07Z WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nVFR conditions through this evening, then a cold front sags south \ninto the area and stalls through Thursday. Showers and possibly a \nthunderstorm are two this evening. Sub-VFR conditions are likely at \ntimes, but it is uncertain if CIGs get any lower than MVFR levels. \nConditions improve Thursday afternoon, with VFR conditions back \nthrough Friday. \n\nA strong cold front moves through Friday afternoon into Friday \nnight. A noticeable increase in winds is expected. Southwest winds \ngust 25-30 knots Friday afternoon, then turn northwest behind the \nfront Friday night.\n\nContinued VFR conditions are expected on Sat, with winds remaining \nout of the NW, but gradually decreasing in magnitude throughout the \nday. VFR Sun with winds becoming light out of the south.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nFavorable marine conditions today as winds remain around 10-15 \nknots. A cold front sags south into the region tonight where it \nstalls. An initial surge of northerly winds could produce SCA \nconditions over the waters tonight into Thursday morning. Winds \nbegin to veer east, then southeast Thursday night. SCA conditions \nare expected to develop across all the waters by Friday morning, \nthen Gale conditions are possible Friday afternoon into\nSaturday. Winds gradually decrease through the day, then become\nlight out of the south on Sunday.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nSouth winds today could result in elevated water levels at sensitive \nlocations along the Chesapeake Bay. Several stations are forecast to \nreach Action Stage, with Annapolis and Havre de Grace forecast to be \ncloser to minor tidal flooding during high tide this evening. A \nsurge of offshore (north) winds tonight will drop water levels.\n\n&&\n\n.CLIMATE...\nBelow are the record highs for today, October 14. \n\n                            Oct 14\nClimate Site                Record High \nWashington-National (DCA)   87F (1975) \nWashington-Dulles (IAD)     86F (1975) \nBaltimore (BWI)             86F (1975) \nAnnapolis (NAK)85F (1975) \nHagerstown (HGR)84F (1975) \nCharlottesville (CHO)94F (1954) \nMartinsburg, WV (MRB)       90F (1928)\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...KRR/CPB\nAVIATION...KRR/CPB\nMARINE...KRR/CPB\n"},"forecast":{"t":1791985340,"days":{"2026-10-14":{"hi":88,"lo":60,"pop":26,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-15":{"hi":66,"lo":58,"pop":77,"short":"Rain Showers"},"2026-10-16":{"hi":82,"lo":48,"pop":61,"short":"Rain Showers Likely"},"2026-10-17":{"hi":56,"lo":39,"pop":8,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-18":{"hi":61,"lo":42,"pop":1,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-19":{"hi":65,"lo":48,"pop":1,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-20":{"hi":68,"lo":49,"pop":2,"short":"Mostly Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1791982800,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,11000,10900,7400,7700,6800,7700,7600,7100,5000,5000,null,4900,4700,4400,4200,3500,2900,2200,2000,1900,1700,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null],"vis":[10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,9.9,9.9,9.9,9.9,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,9.8,9.7,9.5,9.3,9.0,6.0,6.0,6.0,6.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,10.0],"spd":[4,4,5,6,6,7,6,6,5,4,3,3,3,3,3,3,4,4,5,6,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,6,5,5,5,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,4,5,6,7,8],"gst":[8,8,9,10,10,11,11,10,8,7,7,7,7,8,8,9,10,11,12,13,14,13,13,13,13,12,11,11,9,8,8,7,7,7